LOK: a shape made by a command leaves its tool at once

Under the kit a shape command makes its default object directly, then
goes back to the selection tool with an asynchronous dispatch. Until
that user event runs, the construction tool still owns the mouse. A
client that presses on the new object first has the press taken by
the old tool; the queued switch then ends that tool's action and the
release finds nothing held. On macOS, where a client's calls and the
kit's user events reach the main thread from two queues, about one
press in ten on a new connector's end was lost this way (Ely R17.2).
The direct path is not inside the tool's own handler, so under the
kit the switch is dispatched synchronously.

--- a/sd/source/ui/view/drviewse.cxx
+++ b/sd/source/ui/view/drviewse.cxx
@@ -755,7 +755,10 @@
 
     if (bRectangle && !bPermanent)
     {
-        GetViewFrame()->GetDispatcher()->Execute(SID_OBJECT_SELECT, SfxCallMode::ASYNCHRON);
+        // Ely: a kit client's input may beat a queued switch.
+        const SfxCallMode eMode = comphelper::LibreOfficeKit::isActive() ? SfxCallMode::SYNCHRON
+                                                                         : SfxCallMode::ASYNCHRON;
+        GetViewFrame()->GetDispatcher()->Execute(SID_OBJECT_SELECT, eMode);
     }
 }
 
